# Vision dual displays in one window

Scope approved October 31, 2026. Root and Client branches: avp-dual-display,
based on native-builds. The shipping main checkouts stay separate.

Each bookmark selects one or two virtual Host displays. Both resolutions are
independent. Display 1 is left, Display 2 is right, with top edges aligned.
The first implementation presents the full composite in one spatial window.
Separate spatial windows and multiple virtual displays on a macOS Host are
outside this step; the existing Linux Host topology protocol is sufficient.

Use the existing optional secondDisplaySize field and existing Host layout
request. Reject combinations wider than 8192 pixels before saving or launching.
Changing either mode follows the existing authenticated-session closure policy.
Start and retry must carry both modes. Request scaled-span for Vision; retain
separate-displays for the native Mac pilot's multiple local windows.

Use combined width and maximum height for the initial window, then use the
authenticated Host topology. Video, cursor and pointer capture share one
aspect-fit rectangle derived from the decoded composite. Do not split the
encoder, connection, decoder or audio path. Raw Wacom reports retain their
existing device coordinates; do not alter them using spatial-window size.

Validation:
- Saved single and dual bookmarks, edits and return to one display.
- Independent mode order, topology verification, stream dimensions and retry.
- 3840x2160 + 1280x2160, two 2560x2160, two UHD, unequal heights, 8192 boundary
  and rejected larger combinations.
- Pointer mapping at all edges and on both sides of the display seam at
  multiple spatial window sizes; letterbox and malformed coordinates rejected.
- Existing native Mac multiple-screen, tablet and shared component checks.
- Hosted SDK27 device/simulator builds with exact source provenance.
- Actual headset session: both displays visible, readable aspect ratio,
  custom cursor, keyboard, pen pressure, display-seam movement, reconnect and
  returning to one display. Hosted checks do not establish hardware acceptance.

The native baseline has an unresolved reference-image loopback timeout. Retain
that gate and its original deadline/maximum frame size; do not claim a new
TestFlight build until every required native job passes.
